Z||000014||Slammed Fiero||01-10-2001||01:36 PM||||hahah been going out with fierolisa for 6 months ..as of yesterday .... I'm a lucy guy..she likes her fiero's ...plus she's cute...<P>it just makes you want to puke doesn't it?<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...||||reg||1
Z||000015||Will||01-10-2001||04:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lowCG:<BR><B>I wouldn't bother with lowered control arms,as the geometry isn't going to change,only the spring location.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Care to elaborate a little bit?<BR>Lowered control arms work like a cross between lowered springs and lowered spindles. They allow full suspension travel without relocating the bumpstops like lowered spindles, but they change the suspension's ride height away from factory height like lowered springs would.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||lowCG||01-10-2001||08:36 PM||||Yeah,if the actual pivot point only moves upwards,then the effective arm then points upwards also;like a slammed suspension,just without bumpstops.I use Cadillac balljoints with longer studs to simulate a dropped spindle,since the upper control arm angle will be changed without negatively changing the way the car handles.I think it improves it a little,but that's another story.||||reg||1
